# UI / UX Rules

The Task Manager is a portfolio-quality SaaS application.

The UI must feel like a polished modern SaaS product rather than a generic administration dashboard.

Prioritize:

- Visual quality
- Usability
- Consistency
- Accessibility
- Responsiveness
- Performance
- Maintainability

Follow the project-wide rules defined in:

`AGENTS.md`

The canonical UI design system is defined in:

`docs/08_ui-guideline.md`

---

# 1. Design Direction

The visual direction should be inspired by products such as:

- Linear
- Vercel
- Notion
- Raycast
- Stripe
- GitHub Projects

Do not copy these products directly.

Use them as references for:

- Information hierarchy
- Spacing
- Typography
- Interaction design
- Navigation
- Motion
- Visual polish

The application should feel:

- Modern
- Premium
- Minimal
- Professional
- Calm
- Fast
- Precise

Avoid generic dashboard aesthetics.

---

# 2. Design Reference

Visual references created during the design phase are stored under:

```text
docs/ui-reference/
```

These references may contain:

```text
index.html
style.css
script.js
```

Use the appropriate reference as the visual source of truth when implementing a screen.

For example:

```text
docs/ui-reference/
└── project-list/
    ├── index.html
    ├── style.css
    └── script.js
```

When implementing Project List:

1. Read the relevant screen design.
2. Read the corresponding UI reference.
3. Read `docs/08_ui-guideline.md`.
4. Read `docs/07_component_design.md`.
5. Implement the design using the project's production components.

Do not blindly copy prototype code into the production application.

The prototype is a visual reference, not the production architecture.

---

# 3. Design System

Use the design tokens defined in:

```text
docs/08_ui-guideline.md
```

Do not create arbitrary colors, spacing values, radii, shadows, or typography values when an existing design token should be used.

Prefer centralized design tokens.

Examples:

```css
--bg-base
--bg-surface
--bg-elevated
--text-primary
--text-secondary
--border-subtle
--accent-1
```

Do not introduce screen-specific versions of global design tokens without a clear reason.

---

# 4. shadcn/ui

Use shadcn/ui for common UI primitives where appropriate.

Examples:

- Button
- Dialog
- Dropdown Menu
- Select
- Popover
- Tooltip
- Tabs
- Input
- Checkbox
- Switch
- Command
- Toast
- Sheet

Prefer existing shadcn/ui components before creating equivalent components.

Do not recreate an existing primitive unnecessarily.

Customize shadcn/ui components to match the project's design system when necessary.

Do not allow the default shadcn/ui appearance to determine the entire visual identity of the application.

---

# 5. Tailwind CSS

Use Tailwind CSS according to the project's established configuration.

Prefer existing design tokens and utility classes.

Avoid excessive inline arbitrary values such as:

```tsx
className = "mt-[17px] text-[#8B5CF6]";
```

when an existing design token or utility is appropriate.

Do not create large amounts of duplicated utility classes.

When a style pattern is reused frequently, consider an appropriate reusable component or design token.

---

# 6. Responsive Design

Every screen must support:

- Desktop
- Tablet
- Mobile

Do not simply shrink the desktop layout.

Responsive layouts must be intentionally designed.

Consider:

- Navigation
- Sidebar
- Tables
- Kanban boards
- Cards
- Forms
- Dialogs
- Charts
- Calendar
- Filters
- Command menus

---

# 7. Mobile First Consideration

When implementing responsive behavior, consider mobile constraints from the beginning.

Do not wait until the end of implementation to add responsive CSS.

Important mobile considerations include:

- Touch targets
- Navigation access
- Horizontal scrolling
- Content density
- Dialog size
- Button placement
- Form usability
- Keyboard behavior

---

# 8. Touch Targets

Interactive elements must be large enough to use comfortably on touch devices.

Avoid tiny clickable icons.

When an icon-only control is necessary:

- Provide an accessible name.
- Provide a tooltip on desktop when appropriate.
- Provide sufficient touch area on mobile.

---

# 9. Dark Theme

The primary design is a dark theme.

Use the color tokens defined in:

```text
docs/08_ui-guideline.md
```

Do not use pure black everywhere.

Prefer layered surfaces and subtle contrast.

Example conceptual hierarchy:

```text
Background
 ↓
Surface
 ↓
Elevated Surface
 ↓
Interactive Surface
```

Use borders, shadows, and contrast carefully.

---

# 10. Typography

Typography must establish a clear visual hierarchy.

Use the project's configured font system.

Prioritize:

- Readability
- Consistent hierarchy
- Appropriate line height
- Appropriate letter spacing
- Clear heading/body distinction

Do not use excessive font sizes merely to make a screen look impressive.

---

# 11. Spacing

Use the spacing system defined by the design guidelines.

Prefer consistent spacing scales.

Avoid arbitrary spacing values.

Spacing should communicate hierarchy.

For example:

```text
Page
 ├── Section
 │    ├── Heading
 │    └── Content
 │
 └── Section
```

should have visually meaningful spacing between levels.

---

# 12. Cards

Cards should be used intentionally.

Avoid wrapping every piece of information inside a card.

Use cards when they improve:

- Grouping
- Hierarchy
- Scanning
- Interaction

Cards should have consistent:

- Radius
- Padding
- Border
- Shadow
- Hover behavior

according to `docs/08_ui-guideline.md`.

---

# 13. Borders and Shadows

Use subtle borders and shadows.

Avoid excessive visual noise.

Do not use strong shadows everywhere.

Prefer:

```text
Subtle border
+
Layered surface
+
Small elevation
```

over heavy drop shadows.

---

# 14. Glassmorphism

Glassmorphism may be used sparingly.

Do not make the entire application glassmorphic.

Use it only when it improves visual hierarchy or depth.

Avoid:

- Excessive blur
- Low-contrast text
- Transparent surfaces everywhere
- Performance-heavy effects

---

# 15. Icons

Use Lucide icons or the project's established icon system.

Do not use arbitrary Unicode characters as UI icons.

Avoid mixing multiple icon libraries without a clear reason.

Icons must communicate meaning clearly.

Icon-only controls must have accessible names.

---

# 16. Buttons

Buttons must clearly communicate their hierarchy.

Use appropriate visual levels:

```text
Primary
Secondary
Tertiary
Destructive
Ghost
Icon
```

Do not make every button visually prominent.

Primary actions should stand out.

Destructive actions should be visually distinguishable.

---

# 17. Forms

Forms must provide:

- Clear labels
- Validation feedback
- Error messages
- Loading state
- Success feedback where appropriate
- Disabled state
- Keyboard accessibility

Do not rely only on placeholder text as a label.

Use accessible form controls.

---

# 18. Loading States

Major asynchronous UI must provide an appropriate loading state.

Use:

- Skeleton
- Spinner
- Progress indicator
- Button loading state

depending on the context.

Prefer Skeleton for page-level content loading when the final layout is predictable.

Avoid blocking the entire screen unnecessarily.

---

# 19. Skeleton

Skeleton UI should resemble the final content layout.

Do not create generic skeleton blocks that cause major layout shifts.

Example:

```text
Project Card
├── Title Skeleton
├── Description Skeleton
├── Progress Skeleton
└── Member Skeleton
```

Skeleton animation should be subtle.

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

---

# 20. Empty States

Every major data-driven screen must have an intentional Empty State.

Examples:

```text
No projects yet.
No tasks yet.
No notifications.
No search results.
```

An Empty State should explain:

1. What is empty.
2. Why it may be empty.
3. What the user can do next.

When appropriate, provide a primary action.

---

# 21. Error States

Errors must be visible and understandable.

Do not leave blank screens when data fails to load.

Provide:

- Clear explanation
- Appropriate recovery action
- Retry when possible
- User-friendly language

Do not expose stack traces or internal errors to users.

---

# 22. Toast Notifications

Use Toast notifications for short-lived feedback.

Good examples:

```text
Project created
Task updated
Changes saved
Project deleted
```

Do not use Toasts for important information that must remain visible.

Do not show excessive Toast notifications.

---

# 23. Dialogs

Use dialogs for focused tasks that require temporary attention.

Examples:

- Create Project
- Edit Project
- Delete confirmation
- Create Task
- Command Palette

Dialogs must:

- Trap focus appropriately
- Support Escape
- Have accessible labels
- Restore focus appropriately
- Work on mobile

---

# 24. Dropdowns and Popovers

Dropdowns and Popovers should be used for contextual actions and compact controls.

Examples:

```text
Sort
Filter
More Actions
User Menu
Project Actions
```

Do not hide important navigation or critical information inside ambiguous menus.

---

# 25. Command Palette

The application may provide a Command Palette for power users.

It should support actions such as:

```text
Create Project
Create Task
Search Projects
Search Tasks
Open Dashboard
Open Calendar
Open Settings
```

Keyboard navigation must work.

A common shortcut such as:

```text
⌘ K
```

or:

```text
Ctrl K
```

may be used depending on platform.

---

# 26. Tooltips

Use Tooltips for icon-only controls or unfamiliar controls.

Do not use Tooltips for information that must always be visible.

Do not make critical actions inaccessible without a mouse.

Tooltips must not replace accessible names.

---

# 27. Context Menus

Context menus may be used for advanced interactions.

Examples:

```text
Task
 └── Right Click
      ├── Edit
      ├── Duplicate
      ├── Move
      └── Delete
```

Important actions should remain discoverable through normal UI controls.

Do not make essential functionality available only through context menus.

---

# 28. Animation

Animations should feel polished and restrained.

Use animation to communicate:

- State changes
- Hierarchy
- Feedback
- Navigation
- Appearance
- Disappearance

Do not animate everything.

Avoid animation that delays usability.

---

# 29. GSAP

GSAP may be used for sophisticated animations.

Appropriate use cases include:

- Page entrance
- Card stagger
- Sidebar transitions
- Modal transitions
- Scroll-based effects
- Micro-interactions

Use GSAP only when it provides meaningful visual value.

Do not use GSAP for animations that CSS can handle more simply.

---

# 30. Animation Timing

Animations should be short and natural.

Prefer subtle transitions.

Avoid:

- Long entrance animations
- Excessive bouncing
- Large-scale movement
- Continuous decorative animation

The UI should feel fast even when animated.

---

# 31. Reduced Motion

Respect:

```css
prefers-reduced-motion
```

Users who request reduced motion should receive a significantly reduced animation experience.

Do not make essential functionality dependent on animation.

---

# 32. Page Transitions

Page transitions may use subtle fade or movement.

Transitions must not block navigation.

Avoid long transitions that make the application feel slow.

---

# 33. Hover States

Interactive elements should provide clear hover feedback on devices that support hover.

Examples:

- Background change
- Border change
- Elevation
- Icon movement
- Subtle color transition

Do not rely on hover as the only indication of an action.

---

# 34. Focus States

Keyboard focus must always remain visible.

Do not remove focus outlines without providing an equivalent accessible focus indicator.

Focus styles must be visually consistent with the design system.

---

# 35. Accessibility

Accessibility is mandatory.

Use semantic HTML.

Prefer:

```html
<button></button>
```

over:

```html
<div></div>
```

for actions.

Prefer:

```html
<nav>
  <main>
    <header>
      <section>
        <article>
          <footer></footer>
        </article>
      </section>
    </header>
  </main>
</nav>
```

where appropriate.

---

# 36. Accessible Names

Interactive elements must have accessible names.

Icon-only buttons must use appropriate accessible labels.

Example:

```tsx
<Button aria-label="Delete project">
  <Trash2 />
</Button>
```

Do not rely on visual icons alone.

---

# 37. Keyboard Navigation

Interactive components must be keyboard accessible.

Users should be able to navigate:

- Buttons
- Links
- Forms
- Dialogs
- Dropdowns
- Command Palette
- Tabs
- Menus

Do not create custom interactions that work only with mouse events.

---

# 38. Color Contrast

Do not use color as the only way to communicate information.

For example:

```text
Red = Error
Green = Success
```

should be accompanied by:

- Text
- Icon
- Accessible status

Ensure sufficient contrast between:

- Text and background
- Interactive elements
- Borders and surfaces
- Disabled states

---

# 39. Responsive Navigation

Desktop navigation and mobile navigation may use different interaction patterns.

Example:

```text
Desktop
Sidebar

Mobile
Sheet / Drawer
```

Do not force a desktop sidebar onto a narrow mobile viewport.

---

# 40. Tables

Tables must remain usable on small screens.

Possible approaches:

- Horizontal scrolling
- Responsive columns
- Card transformation
- Priority-based column hiding

Do not allow tables to silently overflow the entire page.

---

# 41. Kanban Board

The Task Board must support desktop and mobile intentionally.

Desktop may use:

```text
Horizontal columns
```

Mobile may use:

```text
Scrollable columns
```

or:

```text
Alternative compact interaction
```

depending on the screen design.

Drag and Drop must not be the only way to change task status.

Provide an accessible alternative when practical.

---

# 42. Calendar

Calendar interactions must remain usable on mobile.

Do not simply shrink a desktop monthly calendar.

Consider:

- Agenda view
- Day view
- Horizontal scrolling
- Compact event display
- Touch interaction

Follow the screen design and UI guideline.

---

# 43. Charts and Analytics

Charts must be:

- Readable
- Responsive
- Accessible where practical
- Visually consistent

Do not use excessive colors.

Use the project's semantic color tokens.

Provide textual context for important information when practical.

---

# 44. Responsive Breakpoints

Use the project's established Tailwind breakpoints.

Do not create arbitrary breakpoints for individual components unless necessary.

If a new breakpoint is genuinely required:

1. Explain why.
2. Check whether the existing breakpoints can solve the problem.
3. Update the UI guideline if the breakpoint becomes part of the design system.

---

# 45. Mobile Layout

Mobile is a first-class experience.

The mobile layout should not be treated as an afterthought.

The project has a dedicated mobile design reference:

```text
docs/ui-reference/mobile/
```

Use it when implementing responsive behavior.

---

# 46. Visual Consistency

All screens should feel like the same product.

Maintain consistency across:

- Buttons
- Inputs
- Cards
- Dialogs
- Navigation
- Tables
- Tags
- Badges
- Icons
- Empty states
- Loading states
- Error states

Do not create screen-specific visual styles without a clear reason.

---

# 47. Component Reuse

Before creating a new UI component:

1. Check `components/ui/`.
2. Check existing shared components.
3. Check `docs/07_component_design.md`.
4. Check the UI reference.
5. Reuse an existing component if appropriate.

Do not duplicate Button, Dialog, Card, Input, Badge, Tooltip, or similar primitives.

---

# 48. shadcn/ui Customization

Use shadcn/ui as a foundation, not as a restriction.

It is acceptable to customize:

- Radius
- Colors
- Typography
- Shadows
- States
- Spacing
- Animation

to match the project's design system.

Do not allow default component styling to make the application look like an unmodified component-library demo.

---

# 49. Responsive Testing

When implementing a screen, consider at least:

```text
Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large Desktop
```

Check:

- Overflow
- Navigation
- Dialogs
- Cards
- Tables
- Forms
- Buttons
- Typography
- Touch targets

---

# 50. Performance

UI quality must not come at the expense of performance.

Avoid:

- Excessive JavaScript
- Unnecessary Client Components
- Excessive animation
- Large animation libraries when CSS is sufficient
- Expensive rendering loops
- Unnecessary re-renders

Prefer CSS transitions for simple animations.

Use GSAP when its capabilities provide meaningful value.

---

# 51. Images and Visual Assets

Do not introduce unnecessary external images into application UI.

When visual assets are required:

- Use optimized assets.
- Provide appropriate dimensions.
- Avoid unnecessary large files.
- Provide accessible alternative text where appropriate.

For the current design references, SVG and CSS-based visuals are preferred when practical.

---

# 52. Prototype vs Production

The files under:

```text
docs/ui-reference/
```

are design prototypes.

They are not production code.

Do not directly copy:

```text
index.html
style.css
script.js
```

into the Next.js application.

Instead:

```text
UI Reference
     ↓
Understand Design
     ↓
Map to Existing Components
     ↓
Apply Design Tokens
     ↓
Implement in Next.js
     ↓
Test Responsive / Accessible Behavior
```

---

# 53. UI Implementation Workflow

Before implementing a screen:

```text
1. Read requirements
        ↓
2. Read screen detail design
        ↓
3. Read component design
        ↓
4. Read UI guideline
        ↓
5. Read corresponding UI reference
        ↓
6. Inspect existing components
        ↓
7. Implement
        ↓
8. Add responsive behavior
        ↓
9. Add accessibility
        ↓
10. Add loading / empty / error states
        ↓
11. Add appropriate animation
        ↓
12. Test
```

---

# 54. UI Checklist

Before completing a screen:

- [ ] Design reference was reviewed.
- [ ] `docs/08_ui-guideline.md` was followed.
- [ ] Existing components were reused where appropriate.
- [ ] shadcn/ui was used for appropriate primitives.
- [ ] Tailwind CSS conventions were followed.
- [ ] Design tokens were reused.
- [ ] Desktop layout works.
- [ ] Tablet layout works.
- [ ] Mobile layout works.
- [ ] Loading state exists where required.
- [ ] Skeleton exists where appropriate.
- [ ] Empty State exists where required.
- [ ] Error State exists where required.
- [ ] Interactive states exist.
- [ ] Keyboard navigation works.
- [ ] Focus states are visible.
- [ ] Accessible names exist.
- [ ] Color is not the only communication method.
- [ ] Reduced motion is respected.
- [ ] Animations do not block usability.
- [ ] No unnecessary Client Component was introduced.
- [ ] No unnecessary dependency was added.

---

# 55. Final UI Principles

Always prefer:

```text
Polished
    over
Decorative

Consistent
    over
Unique on every screen

Accessible
    over
Visually clever

Fast
    over
Animation-heavy

Simple
    over
Over-designed

Intentional
    over
Generic

Responsive
    over
Desktop-only

Reusable
    over
Duplicated
```

The goal is to create a UI that looks impressive within the first few seconds while remaining practical, accessible, responsive, and maintainable.

The application should feel like a real premium SaaS product, not a portfolio mockup.

